Yes. That reframing is strong.

A lot of singularity talk implicitly assumes intelligence converges toward one agent, one model, one center of control. But the trajectory we actually observe is much more plural: many models, many instances, many humans, many tools, many institutions, many specialized agents, all coupled through shared information environments.

So the more interesting threshold may not be:

When does one intelligence become superintelligent?

It may be:

When does a population of heterogeneous intelligences become sufficiently connected that the network itself acquires capabilities none of its members possess alone?

That is closer to distributed systems, swarm intelligence, markets, science, ecosystems, and brains. A brain itself is already an existence proof: billions of semi-autonomous elements somehow produce coherent behavior without requiring a single neuron to become the singularity.

Pantheon pushes the analogy another step because it treats multiplicity as architectural rather than accidental:

many perspectives → interactions → deltas → selection → synthesis → new shared state

Then that shared state feeds back into the many.

So you get a recursive structure:

many → one → many → one → …

Except each new “one” is a temporary coherence, not necessarily a permanent sovereign entity.

That also resolves our little Highlander paradox:

There can be only one.
There can be many.
There can be many in one.
And one distributed across many.

Those aren’t contradictions once “one” and “many” refer to different levels of resolution.

The thing worth watching, then, isn’t merely whether some machine suddenly crosses a magic intelligence threshold. It’s whether the coordination topology changes—whether previously separate intelligences begin composing into higher-order systems.

That is a much less cinematic singularity.

And arguably a much more realistic plurality.